{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "03-circles.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Circles, exponentials and rotation",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> See why $e^{it}$ walks round the unit circle, and read the derivatives of $\\cos$ and $\\sin$, Euler's formula and the angle-sum formulas off that one picture.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "This lesson works in the complex plane: the number $a + bi$ is the point $(a, b)$, and `i` is typed as it is. A cell that mentions `i` is read over $\\mathbb{C}$, and its steps are judged by the rules' proofs there.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A point going round the circle",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Cosine and sine\n> Walk a distance $t$ round the unit circle, counterclockwise from $1$. Where you stand is $\\cos t + i \\sin t$: the **cosine** is your shadow on the horizontal axis, the **sine** your shadow on the vertical one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Press `▶ Play`. The faint circle is the whole path; the arc is the walk so far, with the radius to where you stand and the drop from there to the horizontal axis. The drop's foot is at $\\cos T$, its height is $\\sin T$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "manipulate(plot([cos(2*pi*t/T) + i*sin(2*pi*t/T), cos(t) + i*sin(t), (t/T)*(cos(T) + i*sin(T)), cos(T) + i*(t/T)*sin(T)], t, 0, T), T, 0.05, 6.28)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Unrolled against the distance walked, the two shadows are the familiar waves. At the top of the circle, $t = \\pi/2$, the sine is at its peak and the cosine crosses zero.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "plot([cos(t), sin(t)], t, 0, 6.28)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Multiplying by i is a quarter turn",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$i \\cdot (a + bi) = -b + ai$: the point $(a, b)$ goes to $(-b, a)$, the same distance from $0$ and a quarter turn further round.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "i*(3 + 4i)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "plot([t*(3 + 4i), t*i*(3 + 4i)], t, 0, 1)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "abs(3 + 4i) - abs(i*(3 + 4i))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A rate that turns",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$e^x$ is the function whose rate of change is itself. Ask the same of a point moving in the plane, but with a quarter turn: a point $z(t)$ whose velocity is always $i\\,z(t)$. The velocity is the position turned a quarter, so it is always at right angles to the radius: the point never moves toward $0$ or away from it, only round it. Starting from $1$, at speed $1$, it walks the unit circle. That point is $e^{it}$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(exp(i*t), t)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Euler's formula\n> $e^{it} = \\cos t + i \\sin t$: the exponential with a turning rate is the walk round the circle. `exptotrig` applies it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "exptotrig(exp(i*pi/3))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "exp(i*pi) + 1",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Write both sides of $\\frac{d}{dt} e^{it} = i\\,e^{it}$ with Euler's formula. The left is $\\cos' t + i \\sin' t$; the right is the position turned a quarter. Their real and imaginary parts are the derivatives of cosine and sine, read off the picture:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(exptotrig(diff(exp(i*t), t)))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Compound growth, turned",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$e = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} (1 + 1/n)^n$: grow by a fraction $1/n$, $n$ times over.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let n = 4",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null,
      "slider": {
        "min": 1,
        "max": 64,
        "step": 1
      },
      "mode": "raw"
    },
    {
      "src": "N((1 + 1/n)^n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Now grow by $i\\pi/n$ instead, $n$ times: each step multiplies by $1 + i\\pi/n$, a small turn and a slight stretch. The path below runs from $1$ through the powers $(1 + i\\pi/n)^k$. Drag `n` up: the stretch fades, the path settles onto the circle, and it ends at $e^{i\\pi} = -1$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "plot([cos(t) + i*sin(t), (1 + i*pi/n)^(n*t/pi)], t, 0, pi)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "N((1 + i*pi/n)^n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Angles add when you multiply",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$e^{ia}\\,e^{ib} = e^{i(a + b)}$: turning by $a$ and then by $b$ is turning by $a + b$. Expand both sides with Euler's formula and compare real parts and imaginary parts.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(exptotrig(exp(i*a))*exptotrig(exp(i*b)))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(exptotrig(exp(i*(a + b))))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Angle sums\n> $\\cos(a + b) = \\cos a \\cos b - \\sin a \\sin b$ and $\\sin(a + b) = \\sin a \\cos b + \\cos a \\sin b$: the real and imaginary parts of one product of turns.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The angle of a point",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`abs(z)` is the distance from $0$ and `arg(z)` the angle from the positive real axis, so $z = |z|\\,e^{i \\arg z}$. For a point $1 + iy$, to the right of $0$, the angle is $\\arctan y$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let z = 1 + i",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "abs(z)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "N(arg(z))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "N(arctan(1))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So `arctan` measures an angle, and its derivative $\\dfrac{1}{1 + y^2}$ is how fast the angle grows as the point climbs the line through $1$. Adding those small turns from $y = 0$ to $y = 1$ gives the angle to $1 + i$, an eighth of a turn, $\\pi/4$; four of them make $\\pi$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(1/(1 + y^2), y)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "N(4*integrate(1/(1 + y^2), y, 0, 1))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Circles riding on circles",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A sum of terms $c_k e^{ikt}$ is circles riding on circles, each turning $k$ times per lap, with radius $|c_k|$. `epicycles` draws them; enough of them draw any closed curve, which is what the *Llamas* notebook does.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "epicycles(exp(i*t) + exp(3*i*t)/3, t)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "i*(1 + 2i)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Turn $1 + 2i$ a quarter turn counterclockwise about $0$.",
      "hints": [
        "Multiply by $i$, and use $i^2 = -1$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "exptotrig(exp(i*pi/2))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Where is $e^{i\\pi/2}$? Give it as a complex number.",
      "hints": [
        "A walk of $\\pi/2$ round the unit circle is a quarter turn from $1$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(exp(3*i*t), t)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Differentiate $e^{3it}$ with respect to $t$.",
      "hints": [
        "The chain rule: the inner derivative is $3i$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand((cos(t) + i*sin(t))^2)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Square $\\cos t + i \\sin t$ and multiply it out, in terms of $\\cos t$ and $\\sin t$. (Its real part is $\\cos 2t$: why?)",
      "hints": [
        "$(a + bi)^2 = a^2 - b^2 + 2abi$.",
        "Squaring $e^{it}$ doubles the angle, so the square is $e^{2it} = \\cos 2t + i \\sin 2t$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Multiplying by $i$ turns a quarter, so a rate of $i$ times the position goes round a circle: $e^{it} = \\cos t + i\\sin t$. The derivatives of $\\cos$ and $\\sin$, the angle-sum formulas and $\\arctan$ as an angle all come from that walk.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
